import {makeCards} from './helpers';
const g=(leaf,rows)=>makeCards(['Macroeconomics',leaf],rows);

export const macroeconomics=[
...g('National Accounting & Measurement',[
['GDP','What exactly does GDP measure, and what important things does it leave out?','GDP is the market value of final production within a territory over a period. It is useful for measuring market activity but is not a direct welfare index: nonmarket household work, leisure, distribution, environmental depletion and many quality changes are only partially captured or omitted.'],
['Expenditure Identity','Why does Y = C + I + G + NX not tell you what causes a recession?','The identity organizes final expenditure and must hold by accounting. It does not say whether lower consumption caused lower income or resulted from it. Causal analysis requires behavioral equations, shocks and policy responses beyond the identity.'],
['Income Approach','Why must total production equal total income in national accounting?','Payments for final output become wages, profits, rents, taxes or other incomes to someone. Differences between measured approaches reflect statistical discrepancies, timing and data construction rather than a different economic object.'],
['Value Added','Why does GDP count value added rather than every sale?','Counting intermediate goods and final goods separately would double-count production. Value added subtracts purchased intermediate inputs from gross output so each stage contributes only the new value created there.'],
['Nominal GDP','Why can nominal GDP rise without any increase in real production?','Nominal GDP values current quantities at current prices. Higher prices alone therefore raise it. Comparing production over time requires a price adjustment or volume index.'],
['Real GDP','Why is real GDP an index rather than a literal quantity of “all goods”?','Different goods cannot be physically added meaningfully, and relative prices change over time. Statistical agencies use chain-weighted volume methods to approximate quantity growth while updating price weights.'],
['GDP Deflator','How does the GDP deflator differ from the CPI?','The deflator covers domestically produced final goods and services and uses changing expenditure weights. CPI tracks a consumer basket that includes imports. The two therefore respond differently to trade prices and consumption patterns.'],
['GDP per Capita','Why can GDP per capita rise while the median household feels little improvement?','It is an average. Distribution can shift toward high-income households, and output can grow through depreciation-heavy investment or activities poorly reflected in household disposable income. Median consumption and income can tell a different story.'],
['Purchasing Power Parity','Why are market exchange rates often poor for comparing living standards?','Nontraded goods such as housing and services can be much cheaper in lower-income countries. PPP conversion prices a comparable basket across economies, giving a better measure of local purchasing power than converting everything at financial-market exchange rates.'],
['Gross National Income','How does GNI differ from GDP in economies with large cross-border ownership?','GDP counts production inside the territory; GNI adds income residents earn abroad and subtracts income paid to foreign residents. Countries hosting foreign-owned production can therefore have GDP significantly above resident national income.'],
['Saving','What does national saving measure?','It is income not used for current consumption by households or government. In a closed economy it finances domestic investment; in an open economy saving can also finance investment abroad.'],
['Investment','Why does macroeconomic investment exclude buying existing shares?','National accounts count creation of new productive capital, inventories, structures and certain intellectual property. Purchasing an existing financial asset transfers ownership but does not itself create new productive output.'],
['Depreciation','Why is net investment more informative than gross investment for capital accumulation?','Part of gross investment merely replaces worn-out capital. Net investment subtracts depreciation and therefore measures the change in the productive capital stock more closely.'],
['Government Balance','Why is a budget deficit not automatically new stimulus?','The deficit includes automatic responses to the cycle: tax revenue falls and transfers rise in downturns without policy changes. Analysts distinguish cyclical and structural components when assessing discretionary fiscal stance.'],
['Current Account Identity','Why does the current account equal national saving minus domestic investment?','If a country saves more than it invests domestically, the surplus must finance claims on the rest of the world. If investment exceeds saving, foreign capital finances the gap. The identity links trade and financial flows.'],
['Stock vs Flow','Why is debt-to-GDP a ratio of a stock to a flow?','Debt is measured at a point in time while GDP measures production over a period. The ratio is still useful because current income approximates the resource base available to service obligations, but the units are conceptually different.'],
['Real vs Nominal Variables','Why can nominal contracts create real effects when the price level changes unexpectedly?','Debt, wages and taxes are often fixed in money terms. Unexpected inflation changes their real burden before contracts can adjust, redistributing wealth and potentially changing spending and default.'],
['Chain-Weighted Measures','Why do statistical agencies update expenditure weights rather than use a fixed base year forever?','Consumers and firms substitute when relative prices change. A fixed basket eventually overweights goods whose prices rose and underweights new or cheaper products. Chain indexes reduce this substitution bias.'],
['Output per Worker','Why is output per worker not the same as labor productivity per hour?','Workers can differ in hours supplied. Output per worker rises if employees work longer even with unchanged hourly productivity. Output per hour better isolates production efficiency from labor utilization.'],
['Output per Hour','Why is hourly productivity central to long-run living standards?','Hours per person have physical and social limits. Sustained increases in consumption possibilities therefore depend heavily on producing more value per hour through capital, skills, technology and organization.'],
['Quality Adjustment','Why is inflation difficult to measure when products improve?','A higher price for a much better product is partly quality change rather than pure inflation. Statistical agencies use hedonic and replacement methods, but rapid innovation makes the split inherently uncertain.'],
['Imputed Production','Why does GDP include owner-occupied housing services even though no rent is paid?','Otherwise moving from renting to owning the same home would mechanically reduce GDP. Imputing a rental value keeps the measured service flow more consistent across ownership arrangements.'],
['Informal Economy','Why can measured GDP understate production in economies with large informal sectors?','Unregistered businesses, cash work and subsistence production are difficult to observe. Statistical agencies estimate some activity, but measurement gaps remain and complicate cross-country comparisons.'],
['Green Accounting','What would an environmentally adjusted national account change?','It would deduct depletion or degradation of natural assets and potentially add changes in ecosystem services. Ordinary GDP records extraction as current production without fully subtracting the loss of natural wealth.'],
['Nowcasting GDP','Why do economists estimate current GDP before official data arrive?','National accounts are released with delays and revisions. Nowcasting combines high-frequency indicators such as employment, retail sales, payments and production to infer current activity in real time.']
]),
...g('Consumption, Saving & Investment',[
['Consumption Function','Why can current disposable income predict consumption even in forward-looking models?','Liquidity constraints, precautionary motives and limited access to credit make cash-on-hand relevant even when households care about lifetime resources. The strength of current-income sensitivity varies greatly across households.'],
['Marginal Propensity to Consume','Why is the MPC central to fiscal transmission?','It measures how much additional current income is spent rather than saved. Transfers to households with high MPCs create larger immediate demand effects than equal transfers to unconstrained households with low MPCs.'],
['Permanent Income Hypothesis','Why should consumption react more to a permanent income shock than a temporary one?','A permanent shock changes expected lifetime resources substantially, while a temporary shock can be spread across many future periods through saving. Borrowing constraints make actual responses more immediate than the frictionless benchmark.'],
['Life-Cycle Hypothesis','Why do saving patterns change systematically with age?','Households work, retire and face predictable income changes. The life-cycle model predicts accumulation during working years and decumulation in retirement, modified by bequest motives, pensions, uncertainty and housing.'],
['Consumption Smoothing','What does the Euler equation imply about consumption growth?','At an interior optimum, households equate the marginal utility cost of giving up consumption today with the discounted expected marginal utility of future consumption obtained through saving. Expected real interest rates therefore affect the desired consumption path.'],
['Precautionary Saving','Why can greater uncertainty raise saving even when expected income is unchanged?','With convex marginal utility, downside risk makes future resources more valuable at the margin. Households build buffers against unemployment, medical expenses or income volatility, especially when insurance and credit are incomplete.'],
['Buffer-Stock Saving','Why do some households target a range of liquid wealth rather than smooth perfectly?','Precautionary motives push wealth upward while impatience pushes toward spending. The interaction can produce a target buffer of liquid assets that absorbs temporary income shocks.'],
['Borrowing Constraints','Why do borrowing constraints amplify temporary income shocks?','A household that cannot borrow cannot spread a negative current shock over future income. Consumption must fall immediately, creating high marginal propensity to consume from cash transfers and stronger aggregate feedback.'],
['Hand-to-Mouth Behavior','How can a household with substantial net wealth behave as hand-to-mouth?','Wealth can be illiquid in housing, pensions or businesses while liquid balances remain low. Spending then tracks current income because accessing wealth is costly even though the household is not poor in net-worth terms.'],
['Intertemporal Euler Equation','What would make consumption growth systematically violate the standard Euler equation?','Borrowing constraints, habit formation, present bias, heterogeneous returns, uncertainty or measurement error can break the simple representative-agent condition linking expected consumption growth to real interest rates.'],
['Investment Demand','Why is investment especially sensitive to expectations?','Capital pays off over many future periods. Small changes in expected sales, costs or discount rates can therefore create large changes in present value, making investment more volatile than current consumption.'],
["Tobin's q",'Why should firms invest when market value exceeds replacement cost?','If an additional unit of capital raises the market value of the firm by more than its purchase cost, new investment creates value. Adjustment costs make marginal q rather than average q the theoretically precise object.'],
['User Cost of Capital','What determines the effective price of using one unit of capital?','Financing cost, depreciation, tax treatment and expected capital gains all matter. A lower policy rate need not reduce user cost much if risk premia rise or expected asset prices fall.'],
['Accelerator Model','Why can a small change in output growth create a large investment response?','If firms desire a capital stock proportional to expected demand, slowing growth reduces the need for new capacity sharply even when output is still increasing. Investment responds to changes in desired capital, not just its level.'],
['Marginal Efficiency of Capital','How does the expected return on a capital project interact with market interest rates?','Firms invest while expected risk-adjusted returns exceed financing and opportunity costs. A collapse in expected returns can therefore reduce investment even if rates are falling.'],
['Adjustment Costs','Why do firms not instantly jump to the desired capital stock?','Installing equipment, training workers and reorganizing production are costly. Convex adjustment costs spread investment over time and make investment responsive to expected future conditions rather than only today’s demand.'],
['Irreversibility','Why can uncertainty delay investment even when expected net present value is positive?','If capital is costly to resell, waiting preserves the option to learn before committing. Real-options logic therefore creates an uncertainty premium above the ordinary zero-NPV threshold.'],
['Real Options','What is the value of waiting in an investment problem?','Delaying an irreversible project preserves flexibility when future demand or cost is uncertain. The option to invest later has value, so immediate investment requires a sufficiently high expected payoff to justify giving up that option.'],
['Inventory Investment','Why can small inventory changes matter for the business cycle?','Firms accumulate or liquidate inventories when sales differ from expectations. Because desired inventory stocks respond to expected demand, adjustment can amplify production swings even though inventories are a small share of total wealth.'],
['Residential Investment','Why is housing construction highly interest-sensitive?','Housing is durable and heavily financed with debt. Mortgage rates change affordability and developers’ financing costs, while land-use rules and construction lags determine how much quantity rather than price responds.'],
['Corporate Cash Flow','Why can internal cash flow affect investment even if capital markets are available?','External finance is often more expensive because lenders know less than managers and require collateral or monitoring. Cash-rich firms can therefore invest projects that constrained firms must postpone, revealing financing frictions.'],
['Credit Constraints','How do financing constraints create an investment accelerator?','Falling asset values reduce borrower net worth and collateral, raising external-finance premiums. Lower investment then weakens activity further, generating a balance-sheet feedback loop.'],
['Expected Sales','Why can firms invest more when demand expectations rise even before current sales do?','Capital is chosen for future production. Credible news about future demand raises expected utilization and profitability, bringing investment forward. This forward-looking channel makes expectations a source of current fluctuations.'],
['Animal Spirits in Investment','What is economically meaningful about confidence if it is not directly observable?','Confidence can summarize beliefs about future returns when probabilities are poorly known. To be analytically useful, it should be linked to measurable forecast dispersion, uncertainty, financing or investment behavior rather than invoked as a residual explanation.'],
['Saving–Investment Coordination','Why does desired saving not automatically become domestic investment in an open monetary economy?','Saving can finance foreign assets, sit in liquid claims or coincide with weak investment demand. Interest rates, banks, expectations and international capital flows determine how ex ante plans are reconciled.']
]),
...g('Business Cycles',[
['Business Cycle','What makes a business cycle more than a fluctuation in GDP alone?','Cycles involve co-movement in output, employment, income, investment, credit and often inflation. Different variables lead or lag, and the mechanism can differ across episodes. A useful theory must explain the joint pattern, not one series.'],
['Recession','Why is “two negative quarters” not the economic definition everywhere?','A recession is a broad, sustained contraction in activity. Statistical institutions often use multiple indicators such as employment, income, industrial production and sales. The two-quarter rule is a convenient heuristic, not a universal definition.'],
['Expansion','Why can an expansion continue even when growth slows?','Expansion refers to rising economic activity relative to the previous trough, not acceleration of growth. GDP can keep increasing at a slower rate while the economy remains in expansion.'],
['Output Gap','Why is a negative output gap different from negative GDP growth?','A negative gap means actual output lies below estimated potential. GDP can grow quickly while still below potential during recovery, or contract while remaining above a previously estimated trend.'],
['Potential Output','Why can potential output fall after a severe recession?','Weak investment, bankruptcies, skill loss and lower labor-force participation can damage future capacity. This hysteresis means a demand shock can leave supply-side scars rather than only temporary lost output.'],
['Demand Shock','What joint movement of inflation and output is characteristic of a demand shock?','Positive demand usually raises both output and inflation relative to baseline; negative demand lowers both. Policy can alter the observed pattern, so identification requires accounting for the central bank’s response.'],
['Supply Shock','Why does an adverse supply shock create stagflationary pressure?','Production becomes more costly or capacity falls, reducing output while raising prices. Stabilizing inflation then tends to deepen the output loss, creating a policy tradeoff absent under a pure demand shock.'],
['Technology Shock','How can productivity news affect current investment before technology is deployed?','Expected future productivity raises the anticipated return to capital and may stimulate investment immediately. Whether labor rises or falls depends on wealth, substitution and implementation dynamics.'],
['Financial Shock','What makes a financial shock distinct from an ordinary change in interest rates?','Credit spreads, collateral constraints or intermediary balance sheets move independently of the policy rate. Borrowing conditions can therefore tighten sharply even when safe rates are falling.'],
['Uncertainty Shock','Why can uncertainty reduce activity even without changing expected average profitability?','Irreversible investment and hiring become more valuable to postpone when the range of possible outcomes widens. Households may also save more precautionarily, producing a simultaneous demand slowdown.'],
['News Shock','How can good news about the future create a boom today?','If firms and households believe future productivity or income will rise, they may invest and consume more now. If the news later fails to materialize, the earlier expansion can reverse.'],
['Inventory Cycle','Why can unintended inventories amplify a downturn?','When sales disappoint, firms accumulate unwanted stock and cut future production below current sales to reduce it. That inventory liquidation creates a larger temporary output decline.'],
['Real Business Cycle Theory','What does an RBC model treat as the main propagation mechanism?','Real shocks move productivity and relative returns, and households optimally adjust labor, consumption and investment over time. Nominal rigidities play little role. The framework’s dynamic tools remain influential even though pure RBC explanations are incomplete.'],
['New Keynesian Cycle','What changes when nominal rigidity is added to a dynamic general-equilibrium model?','Demand and monetary shocks affect real output because prices cannot adjust instantly. Expectations and policy rules determine how long the deviation persists and how inflation responds.'],
['Financial Accelerator','Why does borrower net worth make financial conditions procyclical?','Higher asset values improve collateral and reduce external-finance premiums during booms. In downturns the reverse happens, tightening credit when cash flow is already weak and amplifying the original shock.'],
['Balance-Sheet Recession','Why might highly indebted firms use low interest rates to repay debt rather than borrow more?','After an asset-price collapse, restoring net worth can dominate profit maximization at the margin. Private deleveraging depresses demand and weakens conventional monetary transmission until balance sheets recover.'],
['Hysteresis','How can cyclical unemployment become structural?','Long joblessness can erode skills, networks and attachment; weak demand reduces investment and entry. These mechanisms raise future unemployment or lower potential output even after the original shock fades.'],
['Okun’s Law','What does Okun’s law summarize?','It is an empirical relationship between changes in output and unemployment. The coefficient varies across countries and periods because hours, participation, productivity and labor hoarding absorb part of output adjustment.'],
['Leading Indicators','Why can financial and survey variables turn before GDP?','Investment and hiring decisions depend on expectations, while asset prices incorporate future information. Yield curves, orders and confidence can therefore lead realized production, though their predictive relationships are not stable laws.'],
['Recession Amplification','What determines whether a small shock becomes a large downturn?','Leverage, nominal rigidity, network linkages, inventories, confidence and policy constraints can amplify disturbances. The initial shock and the propagation mechanism should be analyzed separately.'],
['Sectoral Reallocation','Why can a recession persist even after aggregate demand recovers?','Workers and capital may need to move across sectors with different skills and locations. Search, retraining and balance-sheet frictions slow the reallocation, creating unemployment even when aggregate spending improves.'],
['Recovery','Why can employment recover more slowly than output?','Firms can raise hours, utilization and productivity before hiring new workers. Uncertainty and fixed hiring costs also make employment adjustment lag production.'],
['Soft Landing','What does a monetary soft landing require?','Demand must slow enough to reduce inflation pressure without producing a large rise in unemployment or financial stress. The difficulty is that policy acts with lags and the amount of slack required is uncertain.'],
['Overheating','What distinguishes genuine overheating from simply strong growth?','Output and labor demand exceed sustainable capacity, creating persistent wage or price pressure and resource bottlenecks. Rapid growth after a deep slump can instead reflect recovery toward potential.'],
['Cycle Dating','Why is identifying peaks and troughs easier after the fact?','Data are revised and different indicators turn at different times. Real-time policymakers therefore operate with uncertain estimates of whether the economy has entered or exited recession.']
]),
...g('Inflation & Unemployment',[
['Inflation','Why is inflation a process rather than a single price increase?','Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level. A one-time jump in energy prices changes the level; persistence requires broader wage, expectation, pricing or nominal-demand mechanisms that continue pushing aggregate prices upward.'],
['Headline Inflation','Why can headline inflation be volatile even when underlying inflation is stable?','Food and energy prices respond quickly to weather, commodities and geopolitics. They matter for households but can obscure the persistence central banks care about when assessing medium-term inflation dynamics.'],
['Core Inflation','What is gained and lost by excluding volatile components?','Core measures can reveal persistent underlying price pressure more clearly, but excluded food and energy are real expenses and can feed into wages or expectations. No single core measure perfectly isolates trend inflation.'],
['Trimmed-Mean Inflation','Why might trimming extreme price changes outperform a fixed exclusion list?','The most volatile items change over time. Trimmed means remove unusually large increases and decreases each period, attempting to estimate the central tendency of the full distribution.'],
['Deflation','Why can broad deflation be contractionary even if lower prices seem beneficial?','Falling prices raise real debt burdens, increase real interest rates for a given nominal rate and can encourage delayed spending. When leverage is high, debt-deflation feedback can weaken both borrowers and banks.'],
['Disinflation','How is disinflation different from deflation?','Disinflation means the inflation rate falls but remains positive; prices continue rising, only more slowly. Deflation means the overall price level is falling.'],
['Stagflation','Why did stagflation undermine a simple stable Phillips-curve view?','The 1970s combined high inflation with weak activity, showing that supply shocks and expectations can shift the inflation-unemployment relationship. Demand alone cannot explain every inflation episode.'],
['Inflation Expectations','How can expectations make temporary inflation persistent?','If workers and firms expect future inflation, they set wages and prices accordingly. That behavior can propagate an initial shock even after the original commodity or supply disturbance fades.'],
['Anchored Expectations','What does it mean for long-run inflation expectations to be anchored?','Agents expect inflation to return near the central bank’s objective despite short-run shocks. Anchoring reduces the need for large wage and price adjustments and can lower the output cost of restoring stability.'],
['Wage–Price Dynamics','When can wages and prices reinforce one another?','Workers seek compensation for past or expected inflation, raising firm costs; firms pass costs into prices, validating further wage demands. Whether a sustained spiral emerges depends on productivity, bargaining, margins and monetary policy.'],
['Phillips Curve','What does a modern Phillips curve relate?','Inflation depends on expected inflation and economic slack or marginal cost, often with supply shocks. The relationship can appear unstable when expectations, institutions or policy responses change.'],
['Expectations-Augmented Phillips Curve','Why does the long-run Phillips curve become vertical in Friedman–Phelps logic?','Once expected inflation adjusts to actual inflation, unemployment returns toward its equilibrium rate. Permanently lower unemployment cannot be bought with a permanently higher but stable inflation rate.'],
['New Keynesian Phillips Curve','Why does expected future inflation affect current inflation?','Firms setting prices today know those prices may remain fixed. They incorporate expected future costs and competitors’ prices, making current inflation forward-looking under staggered pricing.'],
['Unemployment Rate','Why can the unemployment rate fall for a bad reason?','People who stop actively searching leave the measured labor force and are no longer counted as unemployed. Participation and employment-population ratios therefore complement the headline rate.'],
['Labor Force Participation','What drives participation independently of the business cycle?','Demographics, education, retirement, health, family structure, taxes and social norms affect whether people work or seek work. Cyclical discouragement and encouragement add shorter-run movements.'],
['Natural Rate of Unemployment','Why is the natural rate unobservable and time-varying?','It depends on matching efficiency, demographics, bargaining, benefits and institutions. Economists infer it from inflation and labor-market behavior, so estimates can be revised substantially after structural changes.'],
['NAIRU','How does the NAIRU differ conceptually from a physical minimum unemployment rate?','It is the unemployment rate consistent with stable inflation under a particular model and regime. Lower unemployment may be sustainable if matching improves or participation changes, so the estimate is not a hard floor.'],
['Beveridge Curve','What does the relationship between vacancies and unemployment reveal?','Normally vacancies rise when unemployment falls. An outward shift—high vacancies and high unemployment—can signal worsened matching efficiency or sectoral mismatch rather than merely weak demand.'],
['Search and Matching','Why can unemployment coexist with unfilled vacancies?','Workers and firms need time to find suitable matches, and information is imperfect. Search models make job creation, destruction and matching efficiency explicit rather than assuming an instantaneous labor-market auction.'],
['Job-Finding Rate','Why can unemployment rise sharply even if layoffs barely change?','If unemployed workers take longer to find new jobs, the stock of unemployment accumulates. Flow analysis separates job-finding from separation and often explains unemployment movements better than focusing only on layoffs.'],
['Separation Rate','How do job separations contribute to unemployment dynamics?','More workers moving from employment into unemployment raises the stock directly. Recessions can involve both higher separations and lower finding rates, with their relative importance varying by country and episode.'],
['Okun’s Law','Why is the output-unemployment relationship not mechanically fixed?','Firms can adjust hours, productivity and labor hoarding; workers can enter or leave the labor force. The same output change therefore creates different unemployment responses across institutions and periods.'],
['Wage Rigidity','Why can nominal wages adjust slowly downward?','Contracts, morale, fairness norms, minimum wages and bargaining institutions make cuts costly. Firms may prefer layoffs or slower hiring, allowing demand shocks to change employment rather than wages alone.'],
['Efficiency Wages','How can above-market wages create involuntary unemployment?','Higher wages can improve effort, retention or applicant quality. Firms then resist cutting pay even when unemployed workers would accept less, preventing the market from clearing through wage adjustment.'],
['Labor-Market Slack','Why is unemployment alone an incomplete measure of slack?','Underemployment, hours, participation, job switching, vacancies and wage growth capture additional margins. A tight labor market can have moderate unemployment if participation or matching is changing.'],
['Real Wage','Why can nominal wage growth be strong while workers lose purchasing power?','Real wages adjust nominal wages for the price level. If consumer prices rise faster than pay, real compensation falls even though paychecks are larger in currency terms.'],
['Unit Labor Cost','Why do unit labor costs matter more for inflation than wage growth alone?','They compare labor compensation with productivity. Wages can rise rapidly without increasing unit costs if output per hour rises equally fast. Persistent inflation pressure depends on costs relative to productivity and margins.'],
['Wage Share','What determines the share of income going to labor?','Compensation, productivity, markups, technology, bargaining and sector composition all matter. The labor share is a macro distributional outcome rather than a direct measure of worker welfare.'],
['Inflation Persistence','What makes inflation continue after the original shock disappears?','Backward-looking expectations, staggered contracts, indexation, wage bargaining and accommodative nominal demand can propagate price changes. Persistence is therefore an empirical feature produced by specific mechanisms.'],
['Sacrifice Ratio','What does the sacrifice ratio attempt to measure?','It measures cumulative output or unemployment cost associated with reducing inflation. The ratio depends on credibility, expectations, wage-setting and the nature of the initial inflation process.']
]),
...g('Long-Run Growth',[
['Solow Model','Why does capital accumulation alone fail to generate permanent per-capita growth in the basic Solow model?','Diminishing returns cause each extra unit of capital per worker to add less output. Saving can raise the long-run level of income, but sustained per-capita growth requires ongoing productivity or technological progress.'],
['Capital Accumulation','How does a higher saving rate affect the Solow steady state?','More saving finances more investment and raises capital per worker until extra investment just covers depreciation and population-related dilution. The economy grows faster during transition but eventually returns to the exogenous technology growth rate.'],
['Steady State','What is constant in a growth-model steady state?','Key ratios such as capital per effective worker stop changing, not necessarily total output. With population and technology growth, aggregate output can continue rising while normalized variables remain constant.'],
['Golden Rule','What does the Golden Rule capital stock maximize?','It maximizes steady-state consumption per worker rather than output. Accumulating more capital beyond that point raises depreciation enough to reduce the resources available for consumption.'],
['Conditional Convergence','Why can poorer countries grow faster without all countries converging to the same income level?','Countries with similar saving, population growth, technology and institutions converge toward similar steady states. Different structural parameters imply different destinations, so convergence is conditional rather than absolute.'],
['Absolute Convergence','What stronger claim does absolute convergence make?','Poorer economies should grow faster regardless of structural differences and eventually approach a common income level. Empirically this is too strong across the entire world, though it can fit more similar groups.'],
['Growth Accounting','How does growth accounting decompose output growth?','It attributes growth to measured capital, labor and a residual called total factor productivity, usually weighting inputs by income shares under specific production assumptions. The residual is not pure technology.'],
['Total Factor Productivity','Why is long-run TFP growth economically powerful?','Productivity lets the same measured inputs produce more output and avoids the diminishing returns that limit capital deepening. Small persistent TFP growth differences compound into large income gaps.'],
['Technological Progress','What does “technology” mean in a growth model?','It can summarize knowledge, production methods, management and efficiency that shift what inputs can produce. Treating it as one scalar simplifies analysis but hides the institutions and innovation processes generating it.'],
['Human Capital','Why can education raise growth beyond simply adding more workers?','Skills increase worker productivity and can improve innovation or technology adoption. Human capital may also create spillovers, making social returns exceed private wage returns.'],
['Endogenous Growth','What does endogenous growth theory change relative to Solow?','It explains persistent productivity growth through economic decisions such as R&D, knowledge accumulation or human-capital investment rather than assuming an external technological trend. Policy can therefore affect the long-run growth rate under some models.'],
['AK Model','How does the AK model avoid diminishing returns to reproducible capital?','Output is linear in a broad capital aggregate, so the marginal product of accumulable factors does not vanish. A higher saving rate can therefore permanently raise the growth rate, though the assumption is demanding.'],
['Romer Model','Why does nonrival knowledge create increasing-return possibilities?','An idea can be used by many producers simultaneously without being depleted. Firms may face diminishing returns individually, while economy-wide knowledge spillovers sustain growth.'],
['Knowledge Spillovers','Why does private R&D tend to understate social returns?','Innovators cannot capture every benefit that their ideas create for imitators, workers and future researchers. This wedge supports subsidies, public science and intellectual-property institutions.'],
['Schumpeterian Growth','How does creative destruction enter modern growth theory?','Firms invest in innovations that replace incumbent technologies. Expected monopoly rents reward research, while successful innovation destroys old rents. Entry policy and competition therefore affect both static allocation and growth.'],
['Innovation Rents','Why can eliminating all markups reduce innovation in a growth model?','If inventors cannot appropriate enough of the future surplus created by new products, costly R&D becomes unattractive. The efficient degree of appropriability balances incentive against monopoly distortion.'],
['Institutions and Growth','Through which channels do institutions affect long-run output?','They change returns to investment, entry, innovation, human capital and reallocation. Causality is difficult because richer countries can also improve institutions, so identification matters as much as correlation.'],
['State Capacity','Why can capable states complement private growth?','They enforce contracts, supply infrastructure, collect revenue and maintain public order. Capacity without accountability can also enable predation, so both ability and constraints matter.'],
['Demography and Growth','How does population age structure affect growth mechanically and behaviorally?','A larger working-age share can raise output per capita and saving; aging can reduce labor supply and increase transfer burdens. Fertility, migration and retirement responses determine the long-run effect.'],
['Secular Stagnation','What mechanisms can produce persistently weak demand despite low interest rates?','High desired saving, low investment, demographics, inequality or safe-asset demand can push the natural real rate very low. Monetary policy then encounters the lower bound more frequently.'],
['Misallocation','How can aggregate productivity be low even when firms have access to similar technologies?','Taxes, credit distortions, regulation or market power can prevent capital and labor from flowing toward high-marginal-product firms. Reallocation can therefore raise output without any new invention.'],
['Convergence Clubs','Why might groups of countries converge internally but not globally?','Thresholds in institutions, human capital, geography or technology adoption can create multiple development paths. Economies with similar fundamentals converge toward one another while remaining separated from other groups.'],
['Development Accounting','How is development accounting different from growth accounting?','Growth accounting explains changes over time within an economy; development accounting decomposes level differences across economies into capital, human capital and productivity components.'],
['Ideas vs Objects','Why do ideas create different growth economics from physical capital?','Physical objects are rival: using a machine in one place prevents simultaneous use elsewhere. Ideas are largely nonrival, allowing scale and spillovers that can sustain increasing returns.'],
['Scale Effects','Why did early endogenous-growth models predict controversial links between population and growth?','More researchers can generate more ideas, implying larger economies should grow faster. The weak empirical relationship motivated semi-endogenous models in which population affects idea production without generating permanent scale effects in per-capita growth.'],
['Creative Destruction Cost','Why can faster innovation reduce the value of existing capital?','New technologies shorten the economic life of incumbent assets and skills. Growth therefore creates transition losses and can alter incentives to invest in long-duration capital.'],
['Productivity Frontier','Why does distance from the world technology frontier change growth strategy?','Far-from-frontier economies can grow through adoption and reallocation; frontier economies depend more on innovation. Institutions suited to imitation may not be optimal once the economy approaches the frontier.'],
['Growth Rate vs Level Effect','Why is it important to distinguish a permanent level increase from a permanent growth-rate increase?','A reform can raise productivity once, placing output on a higher parallel path, without changing the long-run slope. Because growth rates compound indefinitely, confusing the two leads to enormous forecasting errors.'],
['Rule of 70','Why do small differences in annual growth become huge over decades?','A quantity growing at g percent roughly doubles in 70/g years. Persistent compounding makes even one percentage point of productivity growth economically transformative over a generation.']
]),
...g('Fiscal Economics',[
['Government Spending','Why does the macro effect of government spending depend on what the economy is doing when spending occurs?','With idle resources and accommodative monetary policy, extra demand can raise output. Near full capacity, spending is more likely to crowd out private activity or raise prices. Composition also affects long-run productive capacity.'],
['Fiscal Multiplier','Why is the multiplier not a universal constant?','It depends on monetary response, slack, household liquidity, imports, exchange rates, duration and the type of spending or tax change. Empirical estimates therefore vary across states and identification methods.'],
['Transfer Multiplier','Why can two equal-sized transfers produce different aggregate demand effects?','Recipients differ in marginal propensity to consume. Liquidity-constrained or low-wealth households typically spend a larger share quickly, so targeting changes short-run transmission even if the fiscal cost is identical.'],
['Tax Multiplier','Why does a tax cut usually have a smaller first-round demand effect than direct government purchases?','Part of the increase in disposable income is saved rather than spent immediately. The eventual multiplier also depends on expectations about permanence, financing and monetary policy.'],
['Automatic Stabilizers','How do progressive taxes stabilize the cycle automatically?','Tax payments rise faster than income in expansions and fall faster in recessions, smoothing disposable income. Transfers such as unemployment benefits add further countercyclical support without new legislation.'],
['Discretionary Fiscal Policy','What are the main timing problems with discretionary policy?','Recognition, legislative and implementation lags can delay action until conditions change. Infrastructure spending can have high long-run value but may be slow to deploy, while transfers act faster.'],
['Crowding Out','Why can government borrowing reduce private investment?','Borrowing can raise interest rates, absorb financial resources or trigger monetary tightening. Under a lower-bound environment with excess saving, those channels can be weak or reversed.'],
['Ricardian Equivalence','What assumptions are required for debt-financed tax cuts to leave demand unchanged?','Households must anticipate future taxes, be unconstrained, care about descendants appropriately and face similar borrowing conditions as the government. Real-world departures from these assumptions make full equivalence unlikely.'],
['Government Debt','Why is public debt an asset to the private sector but a liability to taxpayers collectively?','Government bonds are financial wealth for holders, while future taxes or seigniorage service the obligation. The consolidated effect depends on who holds debt, monetary arrangements and whether borrowing financed productive assets.'],
['Debt Dynamics','What determines whether the debt-to-GDP ratio rises even with no new primary deficit?','The interest-growth differential matters. If the effective interest rate exceeds nominal GDP growth, existing debt tends to compound relative to income; if growth exceeds interest, the ratio can decline with modest deficits.'],
['Primary Balance','Why separate the primary balance from interest payments?','The primary balance measures current revenues minus noninterest spending and therefore the policy contribution to debt dynamics. Interest payments partly reflect inherited debt and market rates.'],
['Debt Sustainability','Why is there no single safe debt-to-GDP threshold?','Sustainability depends on growth, interest rates, maturity, currency denomination, tax capacity, central-bank regime and investor confidence. Countries with similar ratios can face very different refinancing risk.'],
['Fiscal Space','What creates room for additional borrowing during a crisis?','Credible institutions, stable revenue, low financing cost, long maturities, domestic-currency debt and a strong growth base all matter. Fiscal space is state-contingent rather than a fixed number.'],
['Fiscal Dominance','What happens when monetary policy becomes constrained by government financing needs?','If raising rates threatens debt sustainability or forces fiscal adjustment that is politically impossible, the central bank may accommodate inflation instead. Monetary and fiscal regimes therefore jointly determine nominal stability.'],
['Intertemporal Government Budget Constraint','What does the government budget constraint imply over time?','Current debt must ultimately be backed by future primary surpluses, seigniorage or default/restructuring in present-value terms. It is an accounting restriction, not a claim about the exact timing or political method of adjustment.'],
['Debt Monetization','When does central-bank financing become inflationary?','If money creation persistently finances deficits beyond the economy’s demand for money and productive capacity, nominal spending and prices rise. Temporary crisis purchases need not create the same effect if later policy anchors the price level.'],
['Austerity','Why can fiscal consolidation reduce debt ratios less than expected in a depressed economy?','Spending cuts or tax increases can reduce GDP, shrinking the denominator and tax base. If multipliers are high, the near-term debt ratio may rise even while the primary balance improves.'],
['Fiscal Consolidation','What determines whether consolidation succeeds with limited output loss?','Timing, composition, monetary accommodation, external demand and credibility matter. Consolidation during strong private demand is easier than simultaneous deleveraging during recession.'],
['Sovereign Default','Why can a government default even when it could technically raise taxes further?','Repayment is a political and economic choice balancing financing access against domestic costs of taxation, austerity and distribution. Strategic default models therefore treat willingness as well as ability to pay.'],
['Debt Restructuring','What changes in a sovereign debt restructuring?','Maturity, coupons or principal are modified to reduce the present value or near-term burden. The objective is to restore a feasible debt path while limiting banking and market disruption.'],
['Fiscal Rules','Why impose numerical rules on deficits or debt?','Rules can reduce political deficit bias and improve credibility, but rigid ceilings can force procyclical tightening in recessions. Escape clauses and cyclically adjusted targets attempt to preserve discipline with flexibility.'],
['Balanced-Budget Multiplier','Why can equal increases in spending and taxes still raise output in a simple Keynesian model?','Government spends the full additional amount while households reduce consumption only by the taxed amount times their marginal propensity to consume. The result depends on fixed prices and no monetary offset.'],
['Public Investment','Why should productive infrastructure be analyzed differently from current consumption spending?','Infrastructure can raise future private productivity and tax capacity, so its benefit extends beyond immediate demand. Project quality, congestion and maintenance determine whether the long-run return exceeds financing cost.'],
['Fiscal Insurance','How can the state smooth risks that private markets struggle to insure?','Tax and transfer systems pool unemployment, health and lifetime-income risks across people and time. Moral hazard and fiscal cost limit how complete that insurance can be.'],
['Generational Accounting','Why does current debt not by itself reveal which generation pays?','Taxes, transfers and public services vary over the life cycle. Generational accounting estimates the present value of net fiscal burdens across cohorts, though results depend strongly on projections and discount rates.'],
['Tax Smoothing','Why might a government prefer stable tax rates while allowing debt to absorb temporary shocks?','Distortionary tax costs can rise more than proportionally with tax rates. Borrowing during wars or recessions and repaying gradually can spread those costs over time, provided debt remains credible.'],
['Fiscal Multiplier at the Lower Bound','Why can fiscal expansion be unusually powerful when rates cannot fall further?','The central bank is less likely to offset higher demand with tighter rates, and inflation generated by fiscal expansion can lower the real interest rate. Both channels reinforce rather than neutralize the stimulus.']
]),
...g('Modern Macro Frameworks',[
['IS–LM','What does IS–LM get right despite its outdated monetary mechanics?','It captures interaction between desired spending and financial conditions and provides clear comparative statics for demand management. Modern central banks target rates directly and expectations matter, so contemporary models replace the LM curve with a policy rule.'],
['AD–AS','Why can the AD–AS diagram hide important causal differences between shocks?','Many mechanisms can shift either curve. A useful analysis must specify whether demand moved because of rates, credit or fiscal policy and whether supply moved because of productivity, energy or expectations.'],
['IS–MP','Why does an MP curve fit modern central banking better than LM?','Central banks typically choose an interest-rate target rather than a money quantity. The MP schedule represents the policy rule linking economic conditions to the real or nominal rate, while IS captures spending response.'],
['Rational Expectations','What discipline does rational expectations impose on a macro model?','Agents’ forecasts must be consistent with the model’s own probability structure, preventing systematic exploitable forecast errors. This makes expectations endogenous to policy rules but can overstate agents’ information and computational ability.'],
['Lucas Critique','Why are reduced-form policy correlations fragile?','When a policy rule changes, private expectations and decisions change too. Historical coefficients that embed the old regime may therefore fail in the new one. Structural modeling attempts to use more policy-invariant mechanisms.'],
['Dynamic Optimization','Why are Euler equations everywhere in modern macro?','Households and firms compare marginal benefits across time. Euler equations capture optimal intertemporal tradeoffs and link expectations of future prices or returns to current decisions.'],
['Representative-Agent Model','Why can aggregation into one household miss major policy effects?','It removes differences in wealth, liquidity, income risk and marginal propensities to consume. Policies that redistribute cash flow across heterogeneous households can have aggregate effects absent from a representative-agent model.'],
['DSGE','What does DSGE mean conceptually rather than as a software class?','Dynamic means choices evolve over time; stochastic means shocks are uncertain; general equilibrium means markets and decisions are jointly consistent. Specific conclusions depend on the frictions and heterogeneity built into the model.'],
['RBC Model','What is the central counterfactual in a real-business-cycle model?','Fluctuations are largely efficient responses to real shocks under flexible markets. Comparing its predictions with data helps identify which movements require nominal rigidity, finance or other distortions.'],
['New Keynesian Model','What three relationships form the canonical New Keynesian core?','An intertemporal IS equation links demand to the real-rate gap, a Phillips curve links inflation to expected inflation and slack, and a monetary-policy rule determines the nominal rate. Together they model short-run stabilization.'],
['HANK','Why have heterogeneous-agent New Keynesian models become important?','They combine nominal rigidities with realistic distributions of assets and income risk. Monetary and fiscal policy then operate through cash-flow and redistribution channels in addition to the representative-agent real-rate channel.'],
['Search-and-Matching Model','Why is unemployment modeled as a matching process rather than excess labor supply alone?','Jobs take time to create and workers take time to find suitable vacancies. Matching functions, bargaining and vacancy creation explain persistent unemployment and the Beveridge curve.'],
['Financial-Frictions Model','How do financial frictions change ordinary macro propagation?','Borrower net worth and intermediary capital affect borrowing spreads. Asset-price or income shocks then change financing conditions, amplifying investment and output responses beyond frictionless models.'],
['Agent-Based Model','What does an agent-based macro model allow that representative equilibrium models suppress?','Many heterogeneous agents follow explicit behavioral rules and interact through networks, allowing nonlinear emergent dynamics and crises. The challenge is calibration, identification and connecting simulated mechanisms to data.'],
['Stock-Flow Consistent Model','Why is accounting closure especially valuable for financial macroeconomics?','Every asset and liability is recorded across sectors, and every flow changes stocks coherently. This prevents hidden financing assumptions and makes debt accumulation and sectoral balances transparent.'],
['VAR','What does a vector autoregression do without imposing a full structural model?','It models each variable as a function of lags of itself and others, capturing dynamic correlations. Causal interpretation of shocks still requires identifying restrictions beyond the reduced-form VAR.'],
['Structural VAR','What converts a VAR innovation into an economically meaningful shock?','Researchers impose timing, sign, narrative or external-instrument restrictions that separate underlying structural disturbances. Different identification assumptions can produce different impulse responses.'],
['Local Projections','Why are local projections popular for estimating dynamic responses?','They estimate the response at each horizon directly rather than iterating a fully specified VAR. They are flexible and robust to some misspecification but can be noisier.'],
['Impulse Response','What does an impulse response function show?','It traces the expected path of variables after an identified shock while holding the model’s structure fixed. The interpretation is only as causal as the identification of the shock.'],
['Policy Rule','Why is a reaction function part of the model rather than an external assumption?','Private agents anticipate how policy responds to inflation, output or markets. That expected response changes current behavior, so counterfactuals must specify both the shock and the policy regime.'],
['Regime Switching','Why might one set of macro coefficients fail across all periods?','Policy frameworks, volatility and financial structure change. Regime-switching models allow parameters or shock processes to differ across states such as high inflation, crises or normal times.'],
['Nonlinear Macro','When does linear approximation become dangerous?','Near borrowing limits, zero lower bounds, defaults or financial runs, small shocks can trigger discrete changes. Linear models around a normal steady state can miss these thresholds entirely.'],
['Occasionally Binding Constraints','Why are constraints like the zero lower bound analytically difficult?','They bind only in some states, changing the model’s equations and expectations. Solution methods must account for the possibility that today’s behavior depends on future entry into or exit from the constrained regime.'],
['Estimation vs Calibration','What is the tradeoff between estimating and calibrating a structural model?','Estimation quantifies uncertainty and lets data discipline parameters jointly but can be computationally intensive and sensitive to likelihood assumptions. Calibration can use outside evidence transparently but makes statistical model comparison harder.'],
['Model Uncertainty','Why should policymakers use several models rather than one “best” model?','Different models capture different frictions and can fail under regime shifts. Robust policy seeks decisions that perform reasonably across plausible structures rather than optimizing against one fragile specification.']
])
];

export default macroeconomics;
